WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:02.035
[dramatic music]

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:03.436
NARRATOR: Great
whites are the most

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:07.107
feared predator in the ocean.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:11.211
They typically hunt large
mammals, like seals,

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:15.181
sea lions, and whales.

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:19.786
But they are also responsible
for more attacks on humans

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:23.556
than any other shark species.

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:27.227
And that's not all.

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:30.630
When Collier digs into the
details from the other four

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:34.801
incidents in 2014, it
appears they were all

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:38.104
carried out by white sharks.

00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:45.445
So what's making the great
whites of Southern California

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:49.015
suddenly go on the attack?

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:54.687
Good morning.

00:00:54.754 --> 00:00:56.556
Thanks.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:01:00.193
NARRATOR: According to shark
expert Dr. Andrew Nosal,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.429
a great white's
actions are highly

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:06.399
driven by the presence of prey.

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:09.102
And a major food source
for these predators

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:13.173
can be found along the
coast here in abundance.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:16.075
DR. ANDREW NOSAL:
There's a seal.

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:18.545
NARRATOR: Pinnipeds.

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:21.247
A great white's favorite
food are pinnipeds,

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:24.951
like the California sea
lions we see behind us.

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:26.986
And they like them because
they're full of fat and full

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:27.754
of nutrients.

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:34.160
NARRATOR: But there may
be a particular time

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:38.598
when sharks need to gorge
themselves on these pinnipeds--

00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:42.635
right before they make an
incredible annual journey

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:46.673
to a remote and
mysterious location

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:49.442
nicknamed the White Shark Cafe.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:54.714
DR. ANDREW NOSAL:
The White Shark

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:59.352
Cafe is located about halfway
between California and Hawaii--

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:03.289
so right about here in the
middle of the open ocean.

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:06.326
NARRATOR: Dubbed
a pool of nothing,

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:09.095
this unremarkable
patch of sea is

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:12.298
roughly the size of Colorado.

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:16.469
And for some reason, every year
great whites that typically

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:21.875
swim solo gather
here by the hundreds

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.244
as part of the largest
known congregation

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.881
of white sharks in the world.

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:31.351
It's unclear what the purpose
of the White Shark Cafe is.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.486
But one theory is that
this is a place where

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:35.688
mating is actually happening.

00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:38.258
NARRATOR: Not only that,
but these predators

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:42.896
remain here in the middle
of nowhere for months.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:44.864
DR. ANDREW NOSAL: We typically
see the white sharks leaving

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:47.667
California about
November, December,

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:50.537
and then late summer is when we
see them start showing up again

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:53.706
off the coast of California.

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:57.644
NARRATOR: But this long-term
stay seems odd because there

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:02.215
appears to be a distinct lack of
one crucial thing at the cafe--

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.017
food.

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.151
DR. ANDREW NOSAL:
For a long time,

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:07.854
we thought that it
was a desert of sorts.

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:09.789
There was nothing there.

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:13.359
NARRATOR: So if there's nothing
to eat when they arrive,

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:15.395
could great whites
be stocking up

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:23.736
on pinnipeds before the journey
in order to sustain themselves?

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:25.405
DR. ANDREW NOSAL: White
sharks want the easy meal,

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:26.673
so they're going
to go to a place

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.008
where there's a lot of
seals and sea lions.

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:33.813
NARRATOR: A place like
the California coast.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:39.852
So are humans here simply in
the middle of a feeding frenzy?

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:42.655
Is that what's causing this
alarming spike in attacks?

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:50.296
According to Nosal, the answer
lies far below the surface

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:55.301
of the cafe where,
in 2018, scientists

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:59.539
make an unexpected finding
involving the very thing

00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:01.874
that appeared to be absent--

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:02.842
food.

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:03.910
[uplifting music]

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:04.978
DR. ANDREW NOSAL:
Turns out that there's

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:06.379
actually a lot living there.

00:04:06.446 --> 00:04:09.048
There's all kinds of fish and
crabs and things in the water

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.950
there.

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:13.286
NARRATOR: This prey
just lives much

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:16.456
deeper than could previously
be seen on satellite images.

00:04:19.392 --> 00:04:21.194
DR. ANDREW NOSAL: So because
they're feeding there,

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.063
I don't think the white shark
stocking up on food right

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.466
before their migration
is a sound explanation

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:30.036
for the uptick in shark bites.

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:31.838
[dramatic music]

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:36.242
NARRATOR: Something else must be
driving great whites to attack.

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:40.046
And maybe it isn't
what they're hunting,

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:42.815
but what's hunting them.

